0340: SweetFramebuffer: frame queue with three flip buffers, flips as the copies finish, bounded wait in SwapEnd (kern.sweet_fb_queue, default 0)

Needs 0336. No change of behaviour unless switched on, except a third flip buffer taken from the low pool
at start (10.4 MB that nothing else uses) and a thread that sleeps.

How frames reach the panel with flip buffers (measured with 0336: "present 0", "flips by swap 0, by tick
5920"): SwapEnd only records the swap; the 120 Hz timer hands it to present() at its next tick, the GPU
copy goes into the one back buffer, and the flip is written by the first tick that finds the last copy's
fence done. A frame whose copy is still pending when the next one is presented is copied over and never
shown.

With kern.sweet_fb_queue=1 (read-write; boot-arg sweetfb_queue=1):
- a frame is presented in SwapEnd itself: its copy is queued at once, behind the drawing that made it;
- three flip buffers and a queue of (buffer, fence, surfaces): a copy goes into a buffer that is neither
  the one the MDP reads, nor one it may still be reading, nor one of a waiting frame;
- a thread flips the oldest waiting frame as soon as its copy's fence is done (polling every
  kern.sweet_fb_queue_poll_us, 500, while a frame waits), and not before the MDP has taken the previous
  flip (INTF_FRAME_COUNT moved since it was written; twice if written in the last sixteenth of a refresh;
  34 ms if the counter stands still);
- a frame's surfaces stay in use until it is flipped;
- a SwapEnd that finds no free buffer, or kern.sweet_fb_queue_depth (2) frames waiting, waits up to
  kern.sweet_fb_queue_hold_ms (50, boot-arg sweetfb_queue_hold; 200 at most); then the newest waiting
  frame gives up its buffer (counted as "overwritten").
present() is not changed; the queue fills the slot present() takes its back buffer from and takes over
the flip it leaves pending. The switch can be thrown while frames are in flight.

kern.sweet_fb_queue_diag (read only): frames queued, flips (and by the thread), waits in SwapEnd and their
time, timeouts, forced, late flips, polls, depth, SwapEnd-to-flip histogram.

Locking: all queue state is under fPresentLock (a mutex); SwapEnd and the thread sleep with
IOLockSleepDeadline. Lock order unchanged (fPresentLock, then service_lock; fLock is never held when
fPresentLock is taken).

Checked on the host: the queue functions and scanFrom() cut out of the patched file, run against a model of
the GPU, panel, timer and window server (19 scenarios x 300 seeds x 600 frames): no copy into a buffer the
panel reads, no flip over one not taken, frames in order, every surface released.
Measured on the phone (one boot, n=1 per condition, home screen swipes): overwritten frames 94 of 886
(10.6 %) with 0, 0 of 781 with 1 (flips = swaps).

